WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:03.269
In this lesson, you will build the memory infrastructure

2
00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:05.939
that powers everything else in this course.

3
00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:08.641
You will design persistent memory stores

4
00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:10.844
for different agent memory types,

5
00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:14.147
model memory data for efficient retrieval,

6
00:00:14.447 --> 00:00:17.450
and implement a memory manager that orchestrates

7
00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:21.654
how your agent reads, writes, and retrieves memory.

8
00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:23.757
during execution.

9
00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:26.259
All right, let's dive into the code.

10
00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:29.863
In this lesson, you're going to be constructing the Memory Manager.

11
00:00:29.963 --> 00:00:31.765
What you'll cover in this lesson is

12
00:00:31.765 --> 00:00:33.566
a good understanding of the agent stack.

13
00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:35.935
But also what we mean by when we

14
00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:38.071
refer to the Memory Layer and Memory Manager.

15
00:00:38.605 --> 00:00:40.807
You also get an overview of memory operations,

16
00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:43.510
and we'll also look into terms and terminologies

17
00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:47.414
such as memory engineering, memory units, and context engineering.

18
00:00:47.781 --> 00:00:50.550
Overall, we will lead to the implementation

19
00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.353
of a memory manager and memory operations.

20
00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:56.956
The agent stack refers to a bunch of tools and technologies

21
00:00:57.057 --> 00:01:01.161
that work together to enable an AI agent to perform reliably.

22
00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:03.430
and efficiently in production.

23
00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:05.131
The agent stack typically makes up

24
00:01:05.131 --> 00:01:07.100
the layers you see on the screen,

25
00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.103
but for the purpose of this course, we're going to compress it

26
00:01:10.103 --> 00:01:12.806
into three main layers: the Application Layer,

27
00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:15.508
the Data Layer, and the Infrastructure Layer.

28
00:01:15.809 --> 00:01:19.012
But again, because we're talking about AI agents

29
00:01:19.012 --> 00:01:21.214
and looking at things from an agentic perspective,

30
00:01:21.214 --> 00:01:24.284
we can switch out the Data Layer for Memory Layer.

31
00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:27.287
Now, let's look at the Memory Layer in more detail.

32
00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:30.690
It's typically the part of your agent stack that contains

33
00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:33.593
the Memory Core, which we covered in a previous lesson,

34
00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:35.595
and also the Memory Manager.

35
00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:39.032
The Memory Core and a Memory Manager work together

36
00:01:39.032 --> 00:01:41.734
to essentially create memory-augmented agents

37
00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:46.039
that can handle continuous tasks, operate on long-horizon tasks

38
00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:48.041
and adapt and learn to new information.

39
00:01:48.475 --> 00:01:50.577
If we zoom in on the Memory Manager,

40
00:01:50.577 --> 00:01:54.481
we can understand a Memory Manager as an abstraction on the database

41
00:01:54.481 --> 00:01:57.484
where we have different control flows and control logic

42
00:01:57.484 --> 00:02:00.954
to read and write from memory stores we have in our database.

43
00:02:00.987 --> 00:02:04.257
We're going to see an implementation of this further in this lesson.

44
00:02:04.591 --> 00:02:08.561
But before we go there, let's go over some common memory operations.

45
00:02:08.862 --> 00:02:11.564
The memory manager is going to hold abstractions

46
00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:15.068
which will contain methods that hold create,

47
00:02:15.368 --> 00:02:17.670
read, update, and delete methods.

48
00:02:18.037 --> 00:02:20.306
to memory stores within a database.

49
00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.611
Now, these memory stores are actually memory types that we've covered before.

50
00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:28.414
So Conversational, Knowledge Base, Workflow, and Summary

51
00:02:28.581 --> 00:02:31.885
will all have their allocated table within the database.

52
00:02:32.318 --> 00:02:34.554
Now, each of these memory types we

53
00:02:34.554 --> 00:02:36.756
have a different type of storage requirement.

54
00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:40.560
For conversational memory, it's going to be a relational SQL table.

55
00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.164
Whereas for knowledge base and other memory types,

56
00:02:44.164 --> 00:02:46.566
we're going to be storing data in relational form,

57
00:02:46.566 --> 00:02:48.368
but we're going to have a

58
00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:50.470
data type that can hold vector embeddings.

59
00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:53.740
In each and every memory type, we're going to have

60
00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.042
the read and write operations.

61
00:02:56.442 --> 00:02:58.411
This is just to show you examples of

62
00:02:58.411 --> 00:03:00.647
methods you're going to have in your Memory Manager.

63
00:03:00.947 --> 00:03:03.183
Now, there are also update, delete, and

64
00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:05.752
create methods that you can implement as well.

65
00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:09.656
Another thing to cover is a classification of memory operation.

66
00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:14.027
This classification is dependent on how the memory operation is called.

67
00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:17.330
So we've already went over what memory operations are,

68
00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:20.833
which are just create, read, update, delete operations

69
00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:23.436
on tables held within a database.

70
00:03:23.937 --> 00:03:27.307
But we can distinguish these operations into two forms.

71
00:03:27.607 --> 00:03:29.309
The first being deterministic,

72
00:03:29.709 --> 00:03:34.514
which means that we are executing the memory operations programmatically.

73
00:03:34.714 --> 00:03:36.416
When we interact with an agent,

74
00:03:36.616 --> 00:03:39.619
this memory operations that are deterministic

75
00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:42.722
will be executed regardless of any situations

76
00:03:43.022 --> 00:03:47.527
and will be executed based on a fixed schedule or predefined conditions.

77
00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:50.563
The other classification is Agent Triggered.

78
00:03:50.663 --> 00:03:52.465
This is where the memory operations are

79
00:03:52.465 --> 00:03:54.267
provided to the agent as its tools,

80
00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:56.669
and the agent can decide as to when

81
00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:59.072
and where to use these memory operations.

82
00:03:59.472 --> 00:04:02.375
The triggering of these memory operations

83
00:04:02.475 --> 00:04:05.078
is left to the discretion of the AI agent.

84
00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:06.946
Another term you're going to come

85
00:04:06.946 --> 00:04:08.781
across in this course is Memory Unit.

86
00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:11.284
Let's explain what a Memory Unit is.

87
00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:13.720
A Memory Unit can be thought

88
00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:15.888
of as the smallest atomic unit representation

89
00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:18.591
of information or data held within a database

90
00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.693
that is used within an agentic system.

91
00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:24.230
We've already seen an example of Conversational Memory Unit

92
00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:25.765
where we have the timestamp, the

93
00:04:25.765 --> 00:04:27.533
role of the entity having a conversation

94
00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:29.836
and the content of the conversation itself.

95
00:04:30.036 --> 00:04:33.840
Another example of a Memory Unit is a Workflow Memory Unit

96
00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.441
where you would have a row that

97
00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:37.043
have data types such as the content

98
00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:39.545
of the actual workflow,

99
00:04:39.545 --> 00:04:42.248
the type of the workflow, the timestamp,

100
00:04:42.248 --> 00:04:46.853
and also a vector representation of some content of the workflow.

101
00:04:47.053 --> 00:04:50.857
The content of a Workflow Memory Unit would typically be the steps

102
00:04:51.057 --> 00:04:54.560
and the outcome of the steps taken in the execution.

103
00:04:55.061 --> 00:04:58.564
You're going to hear more about workflow in later lessons.

104
00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:01.267
Another key term is Context Engineering.

105
00:05:01.768 --> 00:05:05.571
Context Engineering is a practice of curating specific content

106
00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:07.940
we want to pass into the context window.

107
00:05:08.441 --> 00:05:10.743
This means that we have data sources

108
00:05:10.743 --> 00:05:13.946
which can actually provide several information

109
00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:16.249
that will pass into our context window,

110
00:05:16.249 --> 00:05:19.752
but rather than stuffing all this information into the context window,

111
00:05:19.752 --> 00:05:22.055
we actually think very carefully

112
00:05:22.055 --> 00:05:23.956
about what context we pass in.

113
00:05:24.357 --> 00:05:26.459
The aim of context engineering

114
00:05:26.459 --> 00:05:29.362
is to maximize the value of each token within a context window.

115
00:05:29.862 --> 00:05:32.165
Ideally, we want a high signal-to-noise ratio

116
00:05:32.165 --> 00:05:34.467
for every token within a context window.

117
00:05:34.767 --> 00:05:36.969
This means that we'll be able to

118
00:05:36.969 --> 00:05:39.472
get the desired output and the desired outcome.

119
00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:43.176
The last term you need to take with you is Memory Engineering,

120
00:05:43.576 --> 00:05:46.479
which is the discipline of actually building and maintaining

121
00:05:46.579 --> 00:05:49.682
systems of memory for an AI agent

122
00:05:49.782 --> 00:05:52.285
to enable it to adapt and actually learn.

123
00:05:52.785 --> 00:05:56.556
In Memory Engineering, you're responsible for all the processes and operation

124
00:05:56.756 --> 00:05:58.958
that goes within a memory life cycle.

125
00:05:59.459 --> 00:06:02.261
Now let's look into what Memory Lifecycle is.

126
00:06:02.662 --> 00:06:05.064
A Memory Lifecycle goes through several steps.

127
00:06:05.765 --> 00:06:07.367
First you have a Raw Data

128
00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:09.268
Source, which goes through an ingestion pipeline,

129
00:06:09.469 --> 00:06:12.271
and then is enriched maybe using an embedding model

130
00:06:12.472 --> 00:06:15.274
or an LLM for augmentation of information.

131
00:06:15.675 --> 00:06:17.977
This is then stored in a database

132
00:06:18.678 --> 00:06:22.749
and also stored in different tables representing either short-term or long-term memory.

133
00:06:23.249 --> 00:06:25.351
Now we have the organization of information.

134
00:06:25.651 --> 00:06:28.154
This will involve process such as indexing

135
00:06:28.454 --> 00:06:30.656
and mapping out relationships within information.

136
00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:33.793
Now we'll go into retrieving the information.

137
00:06:34.093 --> 00:06:36.796
A memory can be retrieved with different retrieval strategies.

138
00:06:36.896 --> 00:06:39.499
The common one are text or lexical,

139
00:06:39.599 --> 00:06:42.902
vector, Graph Traversal, or hybrid.

140
00:06:43.136 --> 00:06:47.140
which is a combination of two or more different retrieval strategies.

141
00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:51.043
When we retrieve information within a Memory Lifecycle,

142
00:06:51.144 --> 00:06:54.313
this information which is memory is passed into the LLM.

143
00:06:54.547 --> 00:06:57.417
The output from an LLM can also be used as memory.

144
00:06:57.717 --> 00:07:00.920
It would go through steps such as Serialization and Augmentation

145
00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:04.323
where we enrich the output to be stored in a database.

146
00:07:04.624 --> 00:07:07.727
And then it goes within the cycle of Storage,

147
00:07:07.827 --> 00:07:11.330
Organization, Retrieval, and back into the LLM again.

148
00:07:11.531 --> 00:07:15.034
Now, this is a very simple overview of the Memory Lifecycle.

149
00:07:15.401 --> 00:07:19.305
The memory lifecycle enables a continuous learning cycle

150
00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:23.910
that an AI agent requires to address long horizon task.

151
00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:27.713
Memory Engineering might be a new term that you've just come across.

152
00:07:28.014 --> 00:07:30.316
but it's a combination of existing disciplines

153
00:07:30.583 --> 00:07:33.486
and takes the practice and principles from these disciplines

154
00:07:33.686 --> 00:07:38.591
to enable the efficient implementation of memory operations within AI agents.

155
00:07:39.025 --> 00:07:41.227
For example, Database Engineering.

156
00:07:41.828 --> 00:07:44.030
Within Memory Engineering, we leverage

157
00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:46.032
principles such as ACID transactions,

158
00:07:46.032 --> 00:07:48.534
Persistent Storage, and having an understanding

159
00:07:48.835 --> 00:07:51.838
of store architecture that we can utilize

160
00:07:51.838 --> 00:07:53.539
within Memory Engineering.

161
00:07:53.773 --> 00:07:56.275
Agent Engineering also comes into play here,

162
00:07:56.576 --> 00:07:58.578
as we're building an AI agents,

163
00:07:58.678 --> 00:08:01.380
we do require an understanding of how to engineer this agent

164
00:08:01.481 --> 00:08:03.983
and when and where to put in memory operations.

165
00:08:04.417 --> 00:08:06.786
Machine Learning Engineering is also required

166
00:08:06.786 --> 00:08:08.588
in Memory Engineering.

167
00:08:09.088 --> 00:08:11.891
This is due to the fact that we might actually fine-tune

168
00:08:11.891 --> 00:08:13.793
some of this embedding models

169
00:08:13.793 --> 00:08:15.795
or even some smaller language models,

170
00:08:15.962 --> 00:08:18.764
typical processes such as Model Versioning,

171
00:08:18.865 --> 00:08:20.967
Reranking Pipelines, and Continual Learning

172
00:08:21.067 --> 00:08:23.870
actually come into play within Memory Engineering.

173
00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:26.572
Lastly, information retrieval.

174
00:08:26.572 --> 00:08:29.375
which is a discipline that is concerned

175
00:08:29.375 --> 00:08:31.077
with implementing retrieval strategies

176
00:08:31.077 --> 00:08:32.678
and optimizing retrieval strategies.

177
00:08:33.079 --> 00:08:35.381
It's essentially utilizing Memory Engineering

178
00:08:35.381 --> 00:08:37.583
when we're implementing Vector Indexes

179
00:08:37.583 --> 00:08:40.386
or any other index strategies we want to utilize

180
00:08:40.386 --> 00:08:43.389
for the efficient retrieval of data from a database.

181
00:08:43.723 --> 00:08:47.126
This essentially are the disciplines involved in Memory Engineering.

182
00:08:47.627 --> 00:08:49.829
As you can see, there is nothing new here,

183
00:08:49.829 --> 00:08:52.131
just an intersection of existing disciplines.

184
00:08:52.732 --> 00:08:54.934
Now we're going into Memory Aware Agents,

185
00:08:54.934 --> 00:08:57.136
and this is an important part of this course,

186
00:08:57.236 --> 00:08:58.871
as we are moving away from

187
00:08:58.871 --> 00:09:00.806
memory augmented agent into Memory Aware Agent,

188
00:09:00.806 --> 00:09:03.109
and it's always good to understand the difference.

189
00:09:03.643 --> 00:09:05.845
We started off with naive implementation

190
00:09:05.845 --> 00:09:07.847
of a memory augmented agent

191
00:09:07.847 --> 00:09:09.649
that just had conversational memory,

192
00:09:09.649 --> 00:09:11.851
which means it only had interaction history.

193
00:09:12.151 --> 00:09:15.821
And this allowed us to add an explicit memory type allocation

194
00:09:15.821 --> 00:09:17.823
to our AI agent.

195
00:09:18.724 --> 00:09:22.228
By adding an explicit memory type allocation to our AI agent,

196
00:09:22.528 --> 00:09:25.731
we're able to move into a fully memory augmented agent.

197
00:09:26.032 --> 00:09:28.434
This means we have an agent that can retrieve information

198
00:09:28.434 --> 00:09:31.437
from different memory stores such as the conversational memory,

199
00:09:31.737 --> 00:09:33.739
workflow memory, toolbox memory,

200
00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:37.443
and other forms of agent memory within our database.

201
00:09:37.810 --> 00:09:39.812
But we can take things further.

202
00:09:39.912 --> 00:09:41.614
We can make our AI agent

203
00:09:41.614 --> 00:09:43.616
memory aware by taking a few steps.

204
00:09:43.816 --> 00:09:46.586
The first one is giving the AI agent

205
00:09:46.686 --> 00:09:49.589
an awareness of the memory stores via the system prompt.

206
00:09:50.089 --> 00:09:54.594
The second is giving memory operations as tools to the AI agent

207
00:09:54.794 --> 00:09:58.297
so that the AI agent could store, retrieve, read,

208
00:09:58.397 --> 00:10:00.800
and forget memory at its own discretion.

209
00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:04.003
The next step is given an AI agent

210
00:10:04.003 --> 00:10:06.205
ability to reason through memory life cycle.

211
00:10:06.205 --> 00:10:10.109
And the final step to move into a Memory Aware Agent

212
00:10:10.109 --> 00:10:12.912
is by segmenting the context window

213
00:10:12.912 --> 00:10:15.314
into portions or partitions

214
00:10:15.314 --> 00:10:17.617
allocated for specific memory types,

215
00:10:17.617 --> 00:10:21.220
which we'll see in all the implementation steps in this lesson.

216
00:10:22.321 --> 00:10:24.523
In this section, we're going to be setting up

217
00:10:24.523 --> 00:10:26.926
all the key system components such as the database,

218
00:10:26.926 --> 00:10:29.028
the embedding models, and a vector store

219
00:10:29.028 --> 00:10:32.331
that we require to implement a memory-aware AI agent.

220
00:10:32.798 --> 00:10:36.102
We are going to start with setting up our database.

221
00:10:36.602 --> 00:10:38.804
We're going to load environment variables

222
00:10:38.804 --> 00:10:42.108
so we have access to all the API keys within our environment.

223
00:10:42.408 --> 00:10:45.311
Then we'll set up our Oracle AI database.

224
00:10:45.611 --> 00:10:48.914
create a connection to the database,

225
00:10:49.215 --> 00:10:51.417
and passing in the right parameters.

226
00:10:51.517 --> 00:10:53.819
and then confirm we have an active connection

227
00:10:53.919 --> 00:10:56.622
by seeing a banner of the database.

228
00:10:57.023 --> 00:10:58.491
Once you execute a cell, you

229
00:10:58.491 --> 00:11:00.192
should be able to see an output.

230
00:11:00.393 --> 00:11:02.662
It will go through a confirmation of steps

231
00:11:02.862 --> 00:11:05.164
to successfully connect to the database.

232
00:11:05.564 --> 00:11:07.867
And then you should be able to see

233
00:11:08.067 --> 00:11:10.169
the banner confirming

234
00:11:10.169 --> 00:11:12.672
which Oracle AI Database version you're using.

235
00:11:13.005 --> 00:11:17.510
We're using Oracle AI database 26ai for this lesson.

236
00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:21.814
The second key component we'll be setting up is our embedding model.

237
00:11:22.214 --> 00:11:25.017
We'll be getting our embedding model from Hugging Face,

238
00:11:25.518 --> 00:11:30.222
specifically using the Hugging Face integration within the LangChain library.

239
00:11:30.589 --> 00:11:33.959
We'll be using the sentence-transformers library

240
00:11:33.959 --> 00:11:38.664
and accessing the paraphrase-mpnet-base-v2.

241
00:11:38.964 --> 00:11:40.966
When this cell is executed,

242
00:11:40.966 --> 00:11:43.869
you're going to have an embedding_model on your local machine.

243
00:11:44.136 --> 00:11:47.640
Next, we're going to set up the database tables,

244
00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:51.644
which are key system components we need for our agent memory

245
00:11:51.644 --> 00:11:53.646
and memory aware AI agent.

246
00:11:53.946 --> 00:11:56.282
We're going to set up names for the

247
00:11:56.282 --> 00:11:58.651
tables that represent different forms of agent memory

248
00:11:58.651 --> 00:12:03.651
we covered previously. For example, for conversational memory, we're going to have

249
00:12:04.423 --> 00:12:07.493
the CONVERSATIONAL_TABLE that will be identified by the text

250
00:12:07.593 --> 00:12:10.763
CONVERSATIONAL_MEMORY. For KNOWLEDGE_BASE_TABLE,

251
00:12:10.763 --> 00:12:13.065
we're going to have semantic memory

252
00:12:13.566 --> 00:12:15.234
and so on and so forth.

253
00:12:16.035 --> 00:12:18.738
All the tables names are stored in a list

254
00:12:19.338 --> 00:12:21.941
and assigned to the variable ALL_TABLES.

255
00:12:22.308 --> 00:12:25.111
We'll iterate through the contents of all tables

256
00:12:25.111 --> 00:12:27.580
and drop a table if it exists

257
00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:30.883
as we're running this specific lesson from scratch.

258
00:12:31.851 --> 00:12:34.854
And lastly, we commit the database transaction.

259
00:12:35.354 --> 00:12:37.123
Once you run the cell, you

